<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Sussex</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West Sussex is a county in south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a> as its county town. It became a separate administrative county in 1889, and together with East Sussex it covers the land of the old kingdom of the South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Its landscape includes the South Downs and a coastal plain with resort towns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-sussex.jpg" alt="West Sussex" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AndyScott · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Downs_Way_-_Landale_brothers_view_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>West Sussex has a land area of 1,991 square kilometres (769 sq mi) and is bordered by Surrey to the north, East Sussex to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a> to the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> to the south. The South Downs, a chalk escarpment, cross it from east to west and divide the north from the south, falling sharply into the Weald to the north and more gently towards the south, where a narrow strip of flat land lies between the hills and the coast. The strip widens to the west, where it is broken by Chichester Harbour, a ria. The Rivers Arun and Adur drain most of the county, and its highest point is at Blackdown, on the Lower Greensand ridges along the Surrey border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement goes back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-paleolithic">Lower Paleolithic</a>: the oldest hominin remains known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> were found at Eartham Pit, Boxgrove. Prehistoric monuments include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> burial mounds called the Devil's Jumps and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> hill forts of Cissbury Ring and Chanctonbury Ring. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> conquered the Atrebates, the indigenous Britons, and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fishbourne-roman-palace">Fishbourne Roman Palace</a>, rural villas such as Bignor and roads such as Stane Street. They also used the Weald for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-smelting">iron production</a> on an industrial scale.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> records the foundation of the Kingdom of Sussex in 477, when Ælle arrived at Cymenshore in three ships with his three sons and killed or put to flight the local inhabitants. Most historians regard the story as somewhat of a myth, although archaeology suggests that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> began to settle in the late fifth century. The name Sussex comes from the Old English Sūþseaxe, the South Saxons. The kingdom was absorbed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and became the county of Sussex, which was traditionally divided into six units called rapes.</p><p>By the sixteenth century the three western rapes were grouped informally and had their own Quarter Sessions. They were governed by a separate county council, and Sussex was divided into the administrative counties of East and West Sussex. In 1974 West Sussex became a single ceremonial county under the Local Government Act 1972, and a large part of the rape of Lewes, the Mid Sussex district, was transferred to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>West Sussex is a ceremonial county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-east-england">South East England</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichester">Chichester</a>, a cathedral city, as its county town. Its largest settlement is Crawley, and the largest towns on the coast are Bognor Regis, Littlehampton and Worthing. It contains Gatwick Airport and stately homes such as Goodwood, Petworth House and Uppark, and castles such as Arundel Castle and Bramber Castle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Sussex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The name survives in the modern counties of East and <b>West Sussex</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sussex">Kingdom of Sussex</a></li></ul></section>
